<Base
  title="Why a retry has to be safe to repeat — Christopher Uzochukwu"
  description="A second try only helps if it does not do the work twice. FleetPilot waits 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms, and 800 ms, then stops on the fifth provider failure."
>
  <h1>Why a retry has to be safe to repeat</h1>
  <p class="dateline">Christopher Uzochukwu · 11 November 2026 · Africa/Lagos</p>
  <p>
    Public repository: <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot">github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot</a>.
    The housemates note is 
    <a href="/writing/fleetpilot-reconciliation/">Desired state, one lock, and a short retry</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was at the market preparing a proper meal, and I went to pay for my ingredients. I used the POS. It declined. Then it declined again, and again. Each time, the amount came off my account anyway. Nothing stopped it. In the end I made a transfer, and that went through. But the machine had taken the money three times for one purchase, and I don’t think the machine ever knew it was the same purchase.
  </p>

  <h2>One person, twice</h2>
  <p>
    Dayo and Ayo, in that other note, both came home with rice. Each of them looked at an empty
    cupboard and acted before the other got back. That is two people.
  </p>
  <p>
    A retry is one person, twice, because the first try looked like nothing. The cupboard is the
    same shape. If the first trip already bought the rice and the answer got lost on the way home,
    the second trip should find the rice and stop. It should not buy another bag to be thorough.
  </p>

  <h2>The second apply has to be boring</h2>
  <p>
    FleetPilot is a small simulation of that. The desired fleet sits in PostgreSQL. What currently
    exists sits in a fake provider in the same process. A machine there is only a record.
    FleetPilot does not boot a real server or call a cloud API.
  </p>
  <p>
    The worker is what changes the record. Create, update, and delete are written so a second
    apply can be boring. If the name is already present, create returns success and does not call
    create again. If the name is already gone, delete returns success. An update whose spec
    already matches returns without calling update. The same checks apply when a running job is
    claimed again after 30 seconds without moving.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the trick. A retry asks again. The apply notices when the ask already landed. Without
    that, the retry is just a second request with a nicer name.
  </p>

  <h2>Four waits, then stop</h2>
  <p>
    A provider error is counted. The default budget is five attempts. After the first four
    failures the job returns to pending. The waits are 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms, and 800 ms. The
    fifth provider failure does not wait. The job is marked dead, and the name is free for a later
    reconcile to enqueue a new job if the two sides still differ.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stop keeps a permanent error from asking forever. The idempotent apply keeps the earlier
    asks from stacking records while those four waits run. Four polite delays do not help if each
    one creates another machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    A held lock, or a cancelled context, is not one of those five. Those paths put the job back
    and leave the attempt count where it was. The budget is for the provider failing, not for two
    workers meeting on the same name. The housemates note is about that lock.
  </p>
</Base>
